{#
The card chrome, defined once.

Settings sections and admin panels are the same object visually — a bordered,
rounded surface with a header band across the top and a body under it — and
they were drifting apart because each side hand-wrote the classes. Admin sat on
`bg-pane-soft` with an `h-11 px-5` summary; settings sat on `bg-surface` with a
`px-4 py-3.5` header. Nobody chose that difference, it just accumulated.

They cannot share a TEMPLATE: an admin panel is a native <details> so it can
collapse without JavaScript, and a settings card is a plain <section> that must
not gain button semantics. So they share the CLASSES instead. These macros
return class strings and nothing else, which keeps one definition of what a
card looks like while leaving each side its own element and its own behaviour.

Deliberately Tailwind classes rather than a CSS @utility: `border-line` and
`bg-surface` carry the theme's alpha vars, and hand-rolling their equivalents
in CSS is exactly the drift this file exists to stop.

Usage:
    {% import '_partials/_card_chrome.html.twig' as chrome %}
    <section class="{{ chrome.shell() }}">
        <header class="{{ chrome.band() }}">…</header>
        <div class="{{ chrome.body() }}">…</div>
    </section>
#}

{# The card itself. `overflow-hidden` so a flush body — a divide-y list, a
   header strip — is clipped by the radius instead of squaring off its corners. #}
{% macro shell() %}rounded-pane border border-line bg-surface overflow-hidden{% endmacro %}

{# The header band: heading, optional subtitle, optional trailing control. The
   border-b is what separates it from the body, so a card whose body is flush
   still reads as two parts. #}
{% macro band() %}px-4 py-3.5 border-b border-line{% endmacro %}

{# Body padding, for cards whose content does NOT bring its own inset.

   Most settings bodies are lists or stacked rows that already pad themselves
   with px-4 — those stay FLUSH, because padding them twice puts 32px between
   the card edge and the first word. Use this only for bodies that are bare
   content: a form, a group of controls. #}
{% macro body() %}px-4 py-5{% endmacro %}
